### Summary
When `cpp-httplib`'s server parses an incoming request, it applies percent-decoding to every header value except `Location` and `Referer`. The validity check (`is_field_value`) is run before decoding, so encoded `%0D%0A` passes the check and is then expanded to a literal `\r\n` byte pair inside the stored header value. [RFC 9110 §5.5](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9110#section-5.5) specifies that header field values are octets and are **not** subject to percent-decoding and creates several attack primitives.


### Details
Here, percent decoding is applied to all fields except `Location` and `Referer`.
https://github.com/yhirose/cpp-httplib/blob/600d220c84853f52011ae4b95fc8518742bcdfac/httplib.h#L5017-L5024

The decoded value is stored directly without re-validation.


### PoC
Server Program
```cpp
#include "httplib.h"
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    httplib::Server svr;

    svr.Get("/echo-header", [](const httplib::Request& req, httplib::Response& res) {
        // Read X-Custom and dump byte-by-byte to expose embedded control bytes.
        std::string val = req.get_header_value("X-Custom");
        std::string hex;
        for (unsigned char c : val) {
            char buf[8];
            snprintf(buf, sizeof(buf), "%02X ", c);
            hex += buf;
        }
        std::string body = "X-Custom raw bytes: " + hex + "\nstring: [" + val + "]\n";
        res.set_content(body, "text/plain");
    });

    std::cout << "cpp-httplib PoC server listening on :8080" << std::endl;
    svr.listen("0.0.0.0", 8080);
    return 0;
}
```

Client Program
```
$ curl -sS http://localhost/echo-header -H 'X-Custom: a%0D%0AInjected: b'
X-Custom raw bytes: 61 0D 0A 49 6E 6A 65 63 74 65 64 3A 20 62
string: [a
Injected: b]
```

The bytes `0D 0A` are present inside the stored header value, despite the wire form containing only `%0D%0A`. Two further reproductions:

| Wire form | Server-observed value |
|---|---|
| `Cookie: session%3Dvictim` | `session=victim` (single `=` becomes a name/value boundary) |
| `X-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.4%2C5.6.7.8` | `1.2.3.4,5.6.7.8` (1 IP turns into 2) |
| `X-Custom: a%20b` | `a b` |
| `X-Custom: a%0D%0AInjected: b` | `a\r\nInjected: b` |

### Impact

- **HTTP response splitting / log injection**
   - Application code that reflects the value (e.g., `res.set_header("Echoed", req.get_header_value("X-Custom"))` or logging frameworks) will emit literal CRLF on the wire, allowing the attacker to inject additional response headers (`Set-Cookie:`, cache directives, redirects, etc.) and / or terminate the original response and forge body content.

- **HTTP request smuggling via the bundled client**
   - When `httplib::Client` is used as a forward proxy and the application copies received headers into the proxied request (a common pattern), `write_headers` (the focus of [CVE-2026-21428](https://github.com/yhirose/cpp-httplib/security/advisories/GHSA-wpc6-j37r-jcx7)) writes the already-decoded value directly with embedded CR/LF. This lifts the impact of [CVE-2026-21428](https://github.com/yhirose/cpp-httplib/security/advisories/GHSA-wpc6-j37r-jcx7) from "user-supplied attacker-controlled value" to "any header that flows through the server-side parse" — a substantially larger attack surface.

- **Cookie / X-Forwarded-For boundary confusion**
   - WAFs and reverse proxies inspect the wire-form (`%3D`/`%2C` are non-special characters); cpp-httplib applications inspect the decoded form. A cookie sent as `Cookie: session%3Devil%3B%20admin%3Dyes` is one cookie to the WAF and *two* cookies (`session=evil; admin=yes`) to the application. The same is true for `X-Forwarded-For` chains.

- **Microsoft IIS-style `%uXXXX`**
   - The decoder also handles `%u003D` → `=`, `%u002C` → `,`, etc. This is non-standard (RFC 3986 does not define `%uXXXX`) and amplifies (3) by allowing payloads that even URL-decoding-aware WAFs miss.

The decoded form is what every downstream consumer in the application sees — middleware, routers, business logic, logging, and the bundled HTTP client. Because the decoded value is never re-validated, the attacker can inject any byte (`\0`, `\r`, `\n`, `,`, `;`, `=`, ` `) into a header value and bypass the strict syntactic checks that happened on the wire form. A representative concrete attack:

```http
X-Custom: ok%0D%0ASet-Cookie: pwned%3D1%3B%20HttpOnly
```

Application code that sets a response header from `X-Custom` will end up emitting two response headers, the second of which is an attacker-controlled `Set-Cookie`.


## Suggested Fix

Treat header values as opaque octets. Either:

1. **Remove percent-decoding from `parse_header` entirely** (recommended). Keep `Location` / `Referer` consistent — i.e., never decode any header values. URI components inside values that the application intends to interpret as URLs should be decoded by the application using the existing `decode_path_component` API explicitly.

2. If backward compatibility requires keeping the current behaviour, **re-run `is_field_value` (or, ideally, `_FORBIDDEN_HEADER_CHARS_RE`) on the decoded result** so that `\r`, `\n`, `\0` etc. are rejected after decoding.

## Summary

The cpp-httplib HTTP client forwards stored Basic Auth, Bearer Token, and Digest Auth credentials to arbitrary hosts when following cross-origin HTTP redirects (301/302/307/308). A malicious or compromised server can redirect the client to an attacker-controlled host, which then receives the plaintext credentials in the `Authorization` header.

## Affected Version

- cpp-httplib **0.38.0** (latest as of report date)
- Likely affects all prior versions since redirect following was introduced

## Severity

**High** -- CVSS 3.1: 7.4 (AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:N)

## CWE

- CWE-522: Insufficiently Protected Credentials
- CWE-200: Exposure of Sensitive Information to an Unauthorized Actor

## Ecosystem

C++ (header-only library, distributed via GitHub, vcpkg, Conan, Homebrew, etc.)

## Details

### Root Cause

When `ClientImpl::redirect()` detects a cross-origin redirect (different host or port), it calls `create_redirect_client()` which:

1. **Correctly removes** the `Authorization` header from the `Request` object (httplib.h, lines 12932-12941)
2. **Then calls** `setup_redirect_client()` which **re-attaches all stored credentials** to the new client targeting the foreign host (httplib.h, lines 13006-13017)

When the new client sends the request, `prepare_default_headers()` (line 13109) sees that the `Authorization` header is absent on the request (it was stripped in step 1) and re-injects it from the client's stored credentials (set in step 2).

**The net result: stripping the header was pointless because the credentials are re-applied through a different path.**

### Vulnerable Code Path

```
ClientImpl::redirect()                          [line 12874]
  └─> ClientImpl::create_redirect_client()      [line 12922]
        ├─> strips Authorization from req       [line 12932-12941]  ← looks safe
        ├─> setup_redirect_client(new_client)   [line 12950/12984]
        │     └─> new_client.set_basic_auth()   [line 13007-13008]  ← re-attaches creds
        │     └─> new_client.set_bearer_token() [line 13010-13011]  ← re-attaches creds
        │     └─> new_client.set_digest_auth()  [line 13014-13015]  ← re-attaches creds
        └─> detail::redirect(new_client, req)   [line 12972/12987]
              └─> new_client.send(req)
                    └─> prepare_default_headers(req)  [line 13109]
                          └─> re-injects Authorization from stored creds  ← leaked
```

### Additional Issue: No HTTPS-to-HTTP Downgrade Protection

The redirect handler creates an HTTP or HTTPS client based solely on the `Location` header's scheme (line 12928). There is no check preventing a downgrade from HTTPS to HTTP, meaning credentials originally transmitted over TLS can be silently sent in cleartext after a redirect.

